Context API is a way to share data between components without passing props manually through every level (called "prop drilling").

Problem without Context API

Suppose you have:

App
 └── Parent
      └── Child
           └── GrandChild

If App has user data and GrandChild needs it, you have to pass props through every component:

<App user={user} />
<Parent user={user} />
<Child user={user} />
<GrandChild user={user} />

This becomes messy.

Solution: Context API

Create a global store that any component can access directly.

App
 └── Parent
      └── Child
           └── GrandChild  ← directly gets data


Real-world Uses

Context API is commonly used for:

Authentication (logged-in user)
Theme (Dark/Light mode)
Language settings
Shopping cart data
Global app settings

Prop Drilling : Passing the data (props) through multiple layers of components, from a parent component to a deeply nested child component.


Context API is a React feature that allows sharing data globally across components without passing props manually through every level of the component tree. It helps solve the prop drilling problem.


